Service native boundary validates operation, arguments, selector and timeout.
Parallel ingress permits independent routed calls and management while an older target is busy.
Ordinary Service wrappers use atomic state changes; custom subclass overrides retain normal mailbox ordering.
Prepare captures sources, reserves a generation, starts an uninitialized target and retains it before initialize.
Source, build, spawn and initialize errors leave the current generation unchanged and record a failed candidate.
Readiness sends one message and requires exact typed expected result before promotion.
Route sends once to the selected target and records completion independently of later promotions.
Signals propagate. Missing replies are uncertain; calls are never automatically retried.
Timeout uncertainty keeps a retired generation's ownership until explicit force retirement.
Force requests target shutdown through the control lane, then records forced status and releases ownership.

Invocation cleanup always runs, including unexpected panics; an unexpected interruption retains uncertainty rather than losing tracked ownership.

Preparation uses the configured spawn deadline, not the shorter ordinary peer HTTP timeout.
